Commands:

```bash
tmp=$(mktemp -d /tmp/m7-nginx-test.XXXXXX)
mkdir -p "$tmp/scripts" "$tmp/deploy"
cp scripts/verify-nginx-sse.mjs "$tmp/scripts/"
# copy current conf, then remove one directive in the run stream block
cp deploy/nginx.conf "$tmp/deploy/nginx.conf"
# Use perl to delete the proxy_buffering line only within run block? Simple sed: deletes first occurrence? chat block also has it. Need to target second occurrence.
```

Could just create a minimal broken config containing a run location missing a directive, plus required upstream etc.? The check happens before buildCheckConfig; it only needs the source text for static assertions. But if static assertions fail, it exits before nginx -t. So a minimal file is fine. But `existsSync` and read. Create:

```nginx
http {
  server {
    location ~ ^/api/v1/runs/[0-9a-fA-F-]+/stream$ {
      proxy_pass http://agent-hub-backend;
      proxy_http_version 1.1;
      proxy_buffering off;
      proxy_cache off;
      gzip off;
      proxy_read_timeout 3600s;
      proxy_set_header Connection "";
    }
  }
}
```

Missing proxy_send_timeout → expect ✗ and exit 1. Also test no location.

But ensureEnvironment will pass. The script then reads `$tmp/deploy/nginx.conf` and runs static assertions. Good.

Alternatively, test in-place with a backup: due to cleanup, no. Temp copy is safer. Let's do two scenarios and capture output. Use `sed`/heredoc. This is shell writing temp files, allowed.

Actually the script imported `DEPLOY_CONF` from `dirname(dirname(script))`, so copy to `$tmp/scripts/verify-nginx-sse.mjs`; yes.

Let me run.

